      "prompt_tokens": 18,
      "completion_tokens": 1116,
      "output": "At its most basic level, **water** is a chemical substance with the chemical formula **H₂O**. This means that each molecule of water is made up of two hydrogen atoms bonded to one oxygen atom.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what makes water unique and important:\n\n### 1. The Molecule\nWater is a **polar molecule**. Because oxygen is more \"thirsty\" for electrons than hydrogen, the oxygen end of the molecule has a slight negative charge, while the hydrogen ends have a slight positive charge. This polarity allows water to stick to itself (cohesion) and to other substances (adhesion), which is why water forms droplets and flows.\n\n### 2. The Three States\nWater is unique because it is the only common substance found naturally on Earth that can exist as a **solid, liquid, and gas** at the same time and under the same conditions (near the surface of the Earth).\n*   **Solid (Ice):** When frozen, water molecules form a rigid, crystalline structure.\n*   **Liquid:** This is the state we use for drinking and washing. It flows and takes the shape of its container.\n*   **Gas (Steam/Water Vapor):** When heated, water molecules move so fast they escape into the air.\n\n### 3. Unique Physical Properties\nWater has several properties that make it essential for life:\n*   **Universal Solvent:** Water can dissolve more substances than any other liquid. This allows it to transport nutrients and minerals through the body and the environment.\n*   **High Surface Tension:** Water molecules stick together strongly at the surface, creating a kind of \"skin.\" This allows insects to walk on water and helps plants pull water up from their roots.\n*   **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb a lot of heat without getting hot. This helps regulate the Earth's temperature and keeps the human body from overheating.\n\n### 4. Biological Importance\nWater is the **foundation of life**. It is the primary component of all living things (humans are about 60% water). It is used in cells to:\n*   Transport oxygen and nutrients.\n*   Remove waste.\n*   Regulate body temperature.\n*   Lubricate joints and organs."
